\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The signed dual-cell differential}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For the \vocabk{oriented cellular maps}{fdip-00NL},
##{\covwedge D_k=(-1)^kD_{k-1}\partial_k\qquad(1\le k\le n).}
In particular #{\covwedge D_2=D_1\partial_2}. Thus #{D_2}
identifies #{Z_2(K;A)} with #{Z^{n-2}(K^*;A)} and
#{B_2(K;A)} with #{B^{n-2}(K^*;A)}.
The following local incidence calculation fixes the sign
for the stated orientation convention.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{f} be a codimension-one face occurrence of an
oriented #{k}-cell #{e}. First give #{f} its induced boundary
orientation, so #{[e:f]=1}. Write an oriented frame of #{e}
as #{(n,F)}, with #{n} the outward normal and #{F} a frame
of #{f}; let #{V} orient #{e^*}. The ambient frame is
#{(n,F,V)}. Moving #{n} past the #{k-1} vectors of #{F}
shows that #{f^*} has frame #{(-1)^{k-1}(n,V)}.
The face #{e^*} inside #{f^*} lies toward the interior of
#{e}, so its outward normal there is #{-n}. Its induced
boundary frame is #{(-1)^kV}, since
##{(-n,(-1)^kV)=(-1)^{k-1}(n,V)}
as oriented frames. Therefore #{[f^*:e^*]=(-1)^k[e:f]}.
Changing a chosen orientation changes the corresponding
incidences and dual orientation together, preserving this
identity. Summing signed occurrences also covers periodic
identifications. Evaluating on every #{f^*} gives the
asserted differential identity.}

\p{Since #{D_1} is invertible, #{\covwedge D_2b=0} exactly
when #{\partial_2b=0}. In degree three the identity is
#{D_2\partial_3=-\covwedge D_3}; invertibility of #{D_3}
then identifies the boundary and coboundary images.}

\p{For a concrete incidence in ambient orientation #{(x,y,z)},
orient an #{xy}-plaquette #{e} by #{(x,y)} and its right
edge #{f} by #{+y}. Its primal incidence is #{+1} and
#{e^*} is oriented by #{+z}. The dual face #{f^*} has
orientation #{(-x,z)} because #{(y,-x,z)} is positive.
Its #{e^*} face has outward normal #{-x}, hence boundary
orientation #{+z}: the dual incidence is also #{+1}.
Appending the remaining coordinate directions gives the same
check in every #{n\ge3}.}
}
